INT. TERRAFORM DOME CATHEDRAL - MORNING

A nave of living glass. Rows of saplings in the aisles. Sunlight filters green through the canopy and falls on a stone altar in which a bonsai is rooted.

DR. IMOGEN VASQUEZ-LINDQ, fifties, tends the tree with a tiny pair of clippers. Behind her, MAGISTRATE ODALIE FREMONT, sixties, in a long grey robe, enters through the great door and walks the aisle with unhurried steps.

ODALIE
You're up early.

IMOGEN
The tree is.

ODALIE
It doesn't sleep.

IMOGEN
It does. It just doesn't tell anyone.

Odalie stops beside the altar. She lays a gloved hand on the trunk. Imogen's clippers pause.

ODALIE
The council voted last night.

IMOGEN
I heard.

ODALIE
Then you know.

IMOGEN
They're closing the dome.

ODALIE
Not closing. Reassigning.

IMOGEN
(laughing softly)
That's a lovely word for it.

She resumes clipping. A leaf drops. Odalie catches it in her palm.

ODALIE
She asked me to give you this.

IMOGEN
Who?

ODALIE
The one who planted it.

She opens her hand. In it lies a small brass key. Imogen stares. Clippers fall from her fingers and ring on the stone.

IMOGEN
She said it didn't exist.

ODALIE
She said many things.
